- op: discuss  # 4e0c
  at: '2026-08-14T09:41:46Z'  # 4e0c
  by: agent  # 4e0c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4e0c
  clock: 5  # 4e0c
  nonce: 4e0c  # 4e0c
  message: "RESIDUAL AUDITED. The \"82 unaudited keys\" residual is NOT 82 risky keys, and\nthe audit found one real defect that is in the INSTRUMENT rather than in the\npopulation.\n\nTHE CENSUS, over the live tree with the shipped discriminator (_scan at the\nREPO ROOT -- passing Path(\"packages\") silently returns 0 mutations, because\n_source_files globs \"packages/*/src\" relative to its argument; my first run\ndid exactly that and reported a clean zero, which is this file's own\n\"a clean zero is usually the instrument\" rule firing on me):\n\n  unaudited keys                                   82\n    never post-hoc-mutated anywhere                77\n    post-hoc-mutated in exactly ONE module          5\n    post-hoc-mutated in more than one module        0\n  control: collision_capable_keys() -> concepts, fields, io_boundary,\n           io_mode, io_primitive  (5, all DECLARED)\n\nThe 5 single-mutator keys are access_mode (dataflow.py), handler_name (go.py),\nparent_base_classes (objc.py), referring_paths (ir.py), visibility_signal\n(finalize.py) -- one writer each, no construction writer.\n\nSo under the MEASURED mechanism no unaudited key is collision-capable. The\nresidual is not \"82 keys are at risk\"; it is \"82 keys nobody has declared\",\nand blanket-declaring them single_writer is precisely the false assurance the\n`unaudited` default exists to refuse.\n\nTHE INSTRUMENT'S BLIND SPOTS, enumerated and counted rather than assumed:\n\n  x.meta[<non-literal>] = ...            0 sites\n  x.meta |= ... / augmented assign       0 sites\n  write_meta_key(meta, <non-literal>)    0 sites\n  x.meta.setdefault(...)                 4 sites  (non-destructive by\n                                                   construction -- setdefault\n                                                   writes only when absent)\n  meta=<non-literal> at construction   145 sites  (construction-only; a\n                                                   collision needs a POST-HOC\n                                                   writer, so this cannot\n                                                   produce one)\n  m = x.meta; m[\"k\"] = ...               1 site   <-- THE REAL GAP\n\nTHE ONE REAL GAP, and it is not academic. `_mutation_key` matches only\n`<expr>.meta[\"k\"] = ...`. A local alias is invisible to it:\n\n  framework_patterns.py:1393, strip_test_file_only_concepts()\n      meta[\"concepts\"] = kept        # meta was bound from <record>.meta\n\n`concepts` is DECLARED merge_union, and rule 1 forbids assigning such a key\ndirectly -- it must route through write_meta_key. This site bypasses the\nchokepoint and rule 1 does not object, purely because of the alias shape. The\nmodule is the SAME ONE that produced INV-hazov's own seventh instance.\n\n(Measured per-SCOPE, not per-module: my first alias probe walked from Module\nand so let an alias bound in one function match subscripts in every other,\nreporting 5 -- the identical module-wide-name-scan defect this class already\npaid for once in go.py. Per-scope, and separating a true alias from a\n`dict(x.meta)` COPY, the population is 1 alias + 13 copies. The 13 copies are\nread-modify-write on a fresh dict and are correctly not flagged; they are the\ncontrol that shows the probe is not simply matching everything.)\n\nAND THE BYPASS IS NOT FIXABLE BY ROUTING IT. That site REMOVES concepts (it\nkeeps a filtered subset). write_meta_key's merge_union semantics would UNION\nthe strip back in, so the chokepoint as specified cannot express this writer.\nThe write-discipline vocabulary has add/refine/replace and no REMOVE.\n\nSo the residual splits into three pieces, none of which is \"declare 82 keys\":\n\n  (a) TIGHTEN THE INSTRUMENT: teach _mutation_key the alias shape, so a\n      merge_union bypass cannot hide behind a local name. 1 live site today.\n  (b) DECIDE THE REMOVAL CASE: either add a removal-shaped discipline, or\n      give write_meta_key an explicit remove entry point, or declare that\n      site an audited exception in the module rather than leaving it a\n      silent bypass. This is a vocabulary question, not a code question.\n  (c) The 82 declarations remain optional and stay `unaudited` by default.\n      Declaring them buys a future guard; declaring them WRONG buys a false\n      assurance. (a) and (b) are worth more than all 82."  # 4e0c
