- op: discuss  # 452f
  at: '2026-08-15T09:26:03Z'  # 452f
  by: agent  # 452f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 452f
  clock: 7  # 452f
  nonce: 452f  # 452f
  message: "SATISFIED. PR #353 closes residual (a) and (b). Verified on the MERGED tree:\n\n  check-meta-write-discipline  rc 0 - 5 collision-capable keys, all declared\n  filter_meta_key(...)         removes 1 of 2 concepts, returns the count\n\n(a) THE ALIAS SHAPE. _mutation_key matched only <expr>.meta[\"k\"] = ..., so a\nbare name defeated rule 1 outright. The one live site (framework_patterns's\nstrip_test_file_only_concepts) now routes through the chokepoint, and the\nlinter sees the shape. A COPY IS NOT AN ALIAS: dict(x.meta) builds a fresh\ndict and destroys nothing until assigned back, which is rule 3's business.\nThe filed population held: 1 alias, 13 copies. Positive control run both\nways - with the old assignment restored the checker FIRES, naming the file\nand line; restored, it is clean. A gate that only ever reports clean is not\nevidence it works.\n\n(b) THE REMOVAL CASE - DECIDED, and the decision is that it is a VERB, not a\nDISCIPLINE. This was the open vocabulary question and the three candidate\nanswers were: a removal-shaped discipline value, an explicit remove entry\npoint, or an audited exception. Taking them in turn:\n\n  * A write_discipline describes how several WRITERS COMBINE. `concepts` is\n    genuinely merge_union for its writers - two enrichment passes each\n    contributing concepts must keep both. Declaring the key removal-shaped\n    would be FALSE about those writers and would forfeit the union guarantee\n    INV-virat and INV-zumin were fixed to obtain. Rejected.\n  * An audited exception in the module leaves the bypass invisible to the\n    linter, which is exactly the \"silent bypass\" this residual objected to -\n    an exception list cannot distinguish \"removes deliberately\" from\n    \"assigned directly and nobody noticed\". Rejected.\n  * filter_meta_key() - CHOSEN. A curator is not a competing writer: it runs\n    AFTER the writers and narrows their agreed result. That is a second verb\n    on the same key. Restricted to merge_union keys, because a single_writer\n    or producer_primary key holds one authoritative value and dropping it is\n    the erasure this whole class is about; the refusal is loud, not a quiet\n    no-op. Both rejected alternatives are recorded in the docstring so this\n    is not re-litigated.\n\n(c) THE 82 UNAUDITED KEYS stay unaudited and stay REPORTED. The audit already\nestablished none is collision-capable under the measured discriminator (77\nnever post-hoc mutated, 5 mutated in exactly one module, 0 in more than one).\nBlanket-declaring them single_writer would buy the false assurance the\n`unaudited` default exists to refuse - which is INV-faput's lesson in another\ncostume, and INV-faput is now closed on exactly that principle.\n\nA LATENT BUG IN RULE 3, found by writing the alias test rather than by\nreading. `_scopes` returns the module PLUS every function, but ast.walk from\nthe MODULE descends into every function body - so \"per scope\" was not per\nscope, and a name bound in one function could match a subscript in another.\nThat is the identical module-wide false positive `_scopes` was introduced to\nkill, and it survived only because no module in the tree happened to pair a\nmeta-bearing construction in one function with a wholesale .meta replacement\nin another. It was mine to find because my alias rule reused the same walker;\none bounded walker now serves both rules, so the boundary is defined once\ninstead of depended on twice. Live tree unchanged either way: 5\ncollision-capable keys, all declared.\n\nRATCHET: this change breached +1 (attr-defined - ast.AST has no lineno).\nMine, fixed at source by narrowing to ast.Assign rather than raising the\nbaseline. 672, delta 0.\n\nWHAT WOULD REOPEN THIS: an eighth instance of last-writer-wins on a slot the\ncensus calls single-writer. The census is now tightened twice (chokepoint\ncalls counted, alias shape seen); each tightening has found real sites, so\nthe honest reading is that the instrument is better rather than finished."  # 452f
